It is an amorphous powder, insoluble in water, alcohol and ether, and has both acid and basic properties. Nitrous acid converts it into xanthine. When oxidized by hydrochloric acid and potassium chlorate it yields guanidine, parabanic acid and carbon dioxide.}}

{{smaller block|⟦2800⟧6-Amino-2-oxypurin, an isomer of guanine, is prepared by heating dichloradenine or 6-amino-2·6·8-trichlorpurin, obtained from 2·6·8 trichlorpurin and ammonia (Fischer, ''Ber.'', 1897, 30, p. 2239) with sodium ethylate to 130° C. and reducing the resulting 6-amino-2-ethoxy-8-chlorpurin with hydriodic acid (E. Fischer, ''Ber.'', 1897, 30, p. 2245). 6-Amino-8-oxypurin, another isomer of guanine, is prepared by heating 8-oxy-2·6-dichlorpurin with alcoholic ammonia and reducing the resulting amino-oxy-chlor compound with hydriodic acid (E. Fischer, ''loc. cit.'').}}
